{
  "text": " I just want to really have you just quickly hit us up. I know in Dallas, you and the British heart doctor that has spoken out, Asim Mahaltra, who has been on, we've had him on virtually on this show, but you guys are going to be speaking live in Dallas just a couple of weeks. We are. You know, we've been communicating back and forth, text, email, and actually talking for nearly two years now. Malhotra is really a wonderful case study because as you've probably viewed your audience, his father takes the vaccine, a very former prominent physician, almost certainly has a vaccine related, atherosclerotic cardiovascular event dies. Malhotra, who is promoting the vaccines on Good Morning Britain, whatever, has to come to the realization that, listen, they have big safety issues. I'm talking to him, coaching him. He says, I have to do my own analysis. I have to write my own papers and he does it and he gets them published. And that's very hard to do. I get them great credit. And then he comes out and says, I have to warn people. And so Malhotra is coming. Asim is coming November 29th. We're going to be in the Dallas Arboretum. Anybody at DFW area? If you want your tickets, want to get in there. Definitely www.vaxsafety.org slash events. Really a brilliant event. You're looking at two of the biggest voices in heart health in the world now standing together, discussing COVID and the vaccine. This is change upon us. This is what has to happen. I always say in many ways, these are the birth pangs. I think this COVID thing, whether it's military driven, whatever it ends up being, to me, it's sort of the white knuckle grasp of the Neanderthals of medicine trying to hold on to their relevance as really this paradigm of medicine is shifting. I feel like people like you are that transitional space. You're leading us into, I think, a better world. I really see this as positive in the end, but change is difficult and it's painful. It is so refreshing to see someone that's in as much turmoil, shall we say, as you are, and still smiling, beautiful, and speaking all over the place. It's just truly an honor to know you. Thank you so much.",
